<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guo Shoujing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1231">1231</a>–<a href="/years/1316">1316</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guo Shoujing was a Chinese astronomer, mathematician and hydraulic engineer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. He built new astronomical instruments and observatories and helped create the Shoushi calendar of 1281, one of the most accurate of its time. As an engineer he designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonghui-canal">Tonghui Canal</a>, completed in 1293, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> shipping into the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guo-shoujing.jpg" alt="Guo Shoujing" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shizhao · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guo_Shoujing-beijing.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Grand Canal was extended northwards through Shandong by the Huitong Canal (1289) and linked to Dadu by the Tonghui Canal of the engineer <b>Guo Shoujing</b> (1293), allowing southern grain to reach the capital by water.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese advisers kept real influence, and Chinese experts such as <b>Guo Shoujing</b>, director of the observatory from 1283 and head of the Water Works Bureau from 1292, held technical posts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingtai">Xingtai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Khanbaliq, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,114.48&amp;year=1286" aria-label="Xingtai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 829 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guo Shoujing"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,114.48&amp;year=1286">What was here in 1286? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
